  "id": "president_appoints_new_governors_lieutenant_governors_2026",
  "title": "President Appoints New Governors and Lieutenant Governors",
  "slug": "president-appoints-new-governors-and-lieutenant-governors-2026",
  "summary": "President Droupadi Murmu announced a major reshuffle of Governors and Lieutenant Governors across several Indian states and Union Territories.",
  "content": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>President Droupadi Murmu has announced a significant reshuffle of Governors and Lieutenant Governors across several states and Union Territories of India. The changes include new appointments, transfers and resignations, reflecting periodic administrative restructuring within India’s constitutional framework.</p><h2>Role of the President in Appointments</h2><p>Under the Constitution of India, the President appoints Governors for each state and Lieutenant Governors or Administrators for Union Territories. These appointments are typically made on the advice of the Union Council of Ministers.</p><h2>Governor: Constitutional Position</h2><p>The Governor is the constitutional head of a state and represents the President of India at the state level. Although executive authority is exercised by the elected state government, the Governor performs important constitutional duties.</p><ul><li>Appoints the Chief Minister</li><li>Summons and prorogues the state legislature</li><li>Assents to bills passed by the legislature</li><li>Ensures constitutional governance</li></ul><h2>Lieutenant Governors in Union Territories</h2><p>Union Territories such as Delhi, Puducherry and Ladakh are administered by Lieutenant Governors or Administrators appointed by the President. Their powers vary depending on the constitutional status of the territory.</p><h2>Purpose of Administrative Reshuffles</h2><p>Periodic changes in gubernatorial appointments help maintain administrative efficiency and bring experienced administrators to different regions where their expertise can be utilized.</p><h2>Political and Administrative Context</h2><p>Governor appointments sometimes occur during periods of political transition or before elections to ensure constitutional continuity in governance.</p><h2>Constitutional Provisions</h2><p>The position of Governor is governed by Articles 153 to 162 of the Constitution of India. These provisions define the powers, functions and responsibilities associated with the office.</p><h2>Importance of Neutral Governance</h2><p>Governors are expected to function as impartial constitutional authorities who ensure that democratic processes and constitutional principles are upheld.</p><h2>Exam Perspective</h2><ul><li>UPSC GS-II: Indian Polity</li><li>Topic: Governor and Union Territory Administration</li><li>Articles: 153–162</li></ul><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>The reshuffle of Governors and Lieutenant Governors announced by the President highlights the dynamic nature of India’s administrative system. These appointments play a crucial role in maintaining constitutional governance across states and Union Territories.</p>",
  "category": "Polity and Governance",
  "tags": ["Governor Appointment", "Lieutenant Governor", "Indian Constitution", "Administrative Reshuffle"],
