  "summary": "Andhra Pradesh has launched Project HANUMAN to address increasing human–wildlife conflicts, particularly involving monkeys and other animals affecting rural and urban communities.",
  "content": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>The Government of Andhra Pradesh has launched Project HANUMAN to address the growing problem of human–wildlife conflict in several parts of the state. The initiative focuses on managing interactions between humans and wildlife, particularly monkeys, which have increasingly entered urban and rural settlements.</p><h2>Background</h2><p>Human–wildlife conflict has become a major challenge in many parts of India due to habitat loss, urban expansion and changing ecological patterns. Animals such as monkeys, elephants and wild boars often enter human settlements in search of food and shelter.</p><h2>Launch of Project HANUMAN</h2><p>Project HANUMAN was launched by Andhra Pradesh Deputy Chief Minister Pawan Kalyan on March 3, 2026, coinciding with World Wildlife Day. The project aims to develop scientific and humane methods to manage wildlife populations and reduce conflict with local communities.</p><h2>Objectives of the Initiative</h2><ul><li>Reduce human–wildlife conflict in affected areas</li><li>Implement scientific wildlife management strategies</li><li>Promote coexistence between humans and wildlife</li><li>Protect biodiversity while ensuring community safety</li></ul><h2>Key Measures</h2><p>The project will involve wildlife monitoring, relocation strategies and improved waste management systems to prevent animals from being attracted to human settlements.</p><h2>Importance of Wildlife Conservation</h2><p>India is home to diverse wildlife species and ecosystems. Conservation efforts must balance ecological protection with the safety and livelihood concerns of local communities.</p><h2>Challenges of Human–Wildlife Conflict</h2><p>Human–wildlife conflict can lead to crop damage, property loss and safety risks for both humans and animals. Effective management requires coordinated action between wildlife authorities, local governments and communities.</p><h2>Role of Community Participation</h2><p>Local communities play an important role in wildlife conservation initiatives. Awareness programs and community engagement help reduce conflicts and encourage responsible environmental practices.</p><h2>World Wildlife Day</h2><p>World Wildlife Day is observed annually on March 3 to raise awareness about the importance of protecting wildlife and preserving biodiversity.</p><h2>Exam Perspective</h2><ul><li>UPSC GS-III: Environment and Ecology</li><li>Topic: Human–Wildlife Conflict Management</li><li>Example: Project HANUMAN</li></ul><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Project HANUMAN represents an important initiative aimed at addressing human–wildlife conflict through scientific management and community participation. The programme highlights the importance of balancing wildlife conservation with the needs and safety of local populations.</p>",
