  "title": "Centre Targets ₹27,500 Crore Aviation Monetisation Under NMP 2.0",
  "slug": "centre-targets-27500-crore-aviation-monetisation-nmp-2-0",
  "summary": "The Union Government has set a monetisation target of ₹27,500 crore for the civil aviation sector under the National Monetisation Pipeline (NMP) 2.0 for the period FY26–FY30 to boost infrastructure investment and operational efficiency.",
  "content": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>The Government of India has announced a monetisation target of ₹27,500 crore for the civil aviation sector under the National Monetisation Pipeline (NMP) 2.0 for the financial years 2026 to 2030. The initiative aims to unlock value from existing aviation assets and attract private sector participation in infrastructure development.</p><p>The move is part of a broader strategy to enhance infrastructure financing without increasing fiscal burden.</p><h2>About National Monetisation Pipeline (NMP)</h2><p>The National Monetisation Pipeline is a government initiative launched to monetize brownfield infrastructure assets.</p><ul><li>Focuses on public-private partnerships</li><li>Targets revenue generation from existing assets</li><li>Encourages private investment</li><li>Enhances asset efficiency</li></ul><p>NMP 2.0 expands the scope of monetisation for the next phase of infrastructure growth.</p><h2>Aviation Sector Focus</h2><p>Under NMP 2.0, the aviation sector will see monetisation of:</p><ul><li>Airport terminals</li><li>Air traffic management assets</li><li>Land parcels associated with airports</li><li>Maintenance and cargo facilities</li></ul><p>These measures aim to improve service delivery and operational efficiency.</p><h2>Objectives</h2><ul><li>Mobilize private capital for infrastructure</li><li>Reduce fiscal pressure on government</li><li>Improve airport management efficiency</li><li>Strengthen India’s aviation ecosystem</li></ul><p>Asset monetisation supports long-term infrastructure sustainability.</p><h2>Economic Impact</h2><p>The aviation sector is a critical driver of economic growth.</p><ul><li>Boosts tourism and trade</li><li>Generates employment</li><li>Enhances regional connectivity</li><li>Supports logistics and cargo networks</li></ul><p>Private participation can accelerate modernization and capacity expansion.</p><h2>Policy Context</h2><p>The monetisation target aligns with:</p><ul><li>Gati Shakti National Master Plan</li><li>UDAN Scheme (Regional Connectivity)</li><li>Atmanirbhar Bharat initiatives</li></ul><p>Integrated infrastructure planning strengthens economic competitiveness.</p><h2>Challenges</h2><ul><li>Ensuring fair valuation of assets</li><li>Balancing public interest with private profit</li><li>Regulatory oversight</li><li>Long-term contract management</li></ul><p>Transparent bidding and strong regulatory frameworks are essential.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>The ₹27,500 crore aviation monetisation target under NMP 2.0 reflects India’s commitment to infrastructure-led growth. By leveraging private investment and optimizing asset utilization, the government aims to strengthen the aviation sector while maintaining fiscal prudence.</p><h2>Exam Perspective</h2><ul><li>Initiative: National Monetisation Pipeline (NMP) 2.0</li><li>Sector: Civil Aviation</li><li>Theme: Infrastructure & Economic Policy</li><li>Relevant for UPSC GS-III (Economy & Infrastructure)</li></ul><h2>Sources</h2><p>Ministry of Finance Briefings 2026, Civil Aviation Policy Reports</p>",
